# AI Agent Development Rules for Cursor

## General Python Style
- Use Python 3.10+ syntax
- Type hints for all function signatures
- Explicit imports (no wildcards like `from x import *`)
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Max line length: 100 characters

## LangChain Specifics
- Use @tool decorator for all agent tools
- Include comprehensive docstrings for tools (they become prompts!)
- Use PromptTemplate for system prompts
- Prefer create_react_agent over deprecated AgentType
- Always use verbose=True during development
- Handle parsing errors with handle_parsing_errors=True

## Error Handling
- Use try/except in all tool functions
- Return meaningful error strings (agent needs to understand them)
- Add retry logic for external API calls
- Log errors, don't silently fail

## Testing
- Write pytest tests for all tools
- Mock LLM calls in tests (use unittest.mock)
- Test both success and failure paths
- Keep tests under tests/ directory

## Documentation
- Add module docstrings explaining the 3-layer architecture
- Comment complex logic (especially prompt engineering)
- Use """ for docstrings, # for inline comments
- Explain WHY, not just WHAT

## Dependencies
- Pin major versions in requirements.txt
- Use virtual environments (venv)
- Keep dependencies minimal
- Document any non-obvious package choices

## AI Agent Best Practices
- Design tools to be atomic (one responsibility)
- Make tools idempotent when possible
- Return structured strings (agent parses them)
- Use Chain-of-Thought prompting patterns
- Add memory for context retention

## Code Organization
- Separate layers clearly (Reasoning, Tools, Orchestration)
- Keep main script under 300 lines
- Extract complex logic to utility functions
- Use constants for magic numbers/strings

## Environment
- Never commit .env files
- Use os.getenv() with defaults where sensible
- Document all required environment variables
- Provide .env.example template